# Challenges Encountered

One of the most valuable parts of this project was the troubleshooting process.

Some of the challenges included:

- Validating the Open vSwitch mirror configuration
- Verifying packet capture with tcpdump before introducing additional tools
- Diagnosing incomplete packet captures
- Rebuilding portions of the Arkime configuration
- Resolving Suricata interface naming issues
- Compiling SiLK and YAF from source on Ubuntu 26.04
- Configuring SiLK site configuration files
- Understanding how YAF exports IPFIX and how SiLK converts those records into flow data
- Validating that every stage of the monitoring pipeline was functioning correctly

Working through these issues reinforced an important lesson: effective troubleshooting requires validating every stage independently rather than assuming data is flowing correctly through the pipeline.

---

# Lessons Learned

One of the major lessons from GCIA is that no single tool provides complete visibility into network activity.

Each component answers a different investigative question.

| Tool | Primary Purpose |
|------|-----------------|
| Arkime | Full packet capture |
| Suricata | Threat detection and IDS alerts |
| Zeek | Protocol metadata |
| SiLK | Network flow analytics |

Together, these tools provide multiple perspectives of the same network activity, enabling much more effective investigations than any individual tool could provide alone.

---

# Example Investigation Workflow

A typical investigation in this lab now follows a layered workflow:

```text
Nmap Scan

        │
        ▼

Arkime
(View full session)

        │
        ▼

Suricata
(Check IDS alerts)

        │
        ▼

Zeek
(Analyze protocol metadata)

        │
        ▼

SiLK
(Search large flow datasets)

        │
        ▼

Wireshark
(Deep packet analysis)
```

Each layer contributes unique context, allowing me to move from broad threat hunting to packet-level forensic analysis.

